# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: read-autolisp-diagnostics-leak-host-errors -- undecodable input prints an SBCL stream error; diagnostics name <source>, not the file
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-20

* Symptom

Found while preparing the E04 (read-autolisp) demo of the video series,
on read-autolisp as installed by clautolisp 2.3.24:

#+begin_example
$ printf ';; cp1252\r\n(setq nom "R\xe9sum\xe9")\r\n' > cp1252.lsp
$ read-autolisp cp1252.lsp
ERROR: :UTF-8 stream decoding error on
       #<SB-SYS:FD-STREAM for "file /.../cp1252.lsp" {10019D1D13}>:
         the octet sequence #(233 115 117) cannot be decoded.
$ read-autolisp --external-format bogus cp1252.lsp
ERROR: Undefined external-format: :BOGUS
$ printf '(setq x (+ 1 2)\n' > casse.lsp
$ read-autolisp casse.lsp
ERROR UNEXPECTED-EOF at <source>:2:1-2:1: Unexpected end of input inside list.
#+end_example

1. The first two print the host Lisp's condition text (an SBCL stream
   object, an implementation keyword). clautolisp, on the same file,
   answers =read error: bytes that are not valid in the source encoding
   (choose the encoding with -Esource ENCODING)=.
2. The reader diagnostic names =<source>= instead of =casse.lsp=: the
   source name is not passed to the reader, so with several files on the
   command line an error cannot be attributed.

* To do

- Failing tests first (tools/read-autolisp): a CP1252 file without
  =--external-format= gets a one-line diagnostic naming the file and the
  option to use, exit status 65; an unknown =--external-format= is a usage
  error (64); the diagnostic span carries the file name.
- Reuse the reader/CLI decoding-error path clautolisp already has rather
  than a new message.
- read-autolisp is a shipped program: bump it (AGENTS.md, "A changed
  library is a changed program") and add a RELEASE_NOTES entry.
